    "result_text": "Done. The build is green and the new rule is in place.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe E.164 telephone flows through validation on the generated `OwnerFieldsDto`/`OwnerDto` (bean validation on the request DTO is what produces the `400` here \u2014 an entity-level constraint would instead throw at persist and map to `500`). The repo already has a precedent for custom DTO field validators wired through the OpenAPI spec (`PetAgeValidation` via `x-field-extra-annotation`), so I followed that exact idiom:\n\n1. **`E164NationalLength.java`** \u2014 a new field constraint annotation (mirrors `PetAgeValidation`).\n2. **`E164NationalLengthValidator.java`** \u2014 a small `ConstraintValidator<E164NationalLength, String>`. It normalises the raw input with the existing `PhoneNumbers.toE164`, then checks national-digit count against a `Map.of(\"+61\", 9, \"+1\", 10)`. Blank input and unknown country codes pass through (so `@NotEmpty`/`@Pattern` and other countries keep their existing behaviour).\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added one line, `x-field-extra-annotation: \"@...E164NationalLength\"`, on the `telephone` field so the generator stamps the annotation onto the DTOs.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- **No existing method was modified** \u2014 `Owner`, `PhoneNumbers`, the controllers, and the exception advice are all untouched, so the large-class `WMC_other` multiplier is never paid.\n- The new logic lives in one small, single-method validator class (its `WMC_other` is 0), and `PhoneNumbers.toE164` is **reused** rather than duplicated.\n- The only edit to an existing file is a single additive YAML line \u2014 no function bodies changed.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Full suite: `BUILD SUCCESS`, `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` passes.\n- Confirmed the annotation is emitted into both generated DTOs, and directly probed the validator across cases the old regex can't catch \u2014 e.g. `+6112345678` (8 national digits) and `+1608555102` (9 national digits) are now rejected, while `+61412345678`, `+16085551023`, and unknown codes like `+441234567890` pass.",
